# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Customer data
#
# There is nothing to ignore here any more, and that is the point: everything a
# customer owns now lives in the sibling `../data/` directory, outside this
# repository entirely, so it cannot be committed by accident and cannot be lost
# when this folder is replaced.
#
# The two entries below are belt-and-braces for anyone who sets DATA_DIR to a
# path inside the application tree despite the documentation. See
# docs/DATA-BOUNDARY.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/data/
.snapshots/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment files
#
# `.env` and `.env*.local` are covered at the top of this file. This widens the
# net to every other variant an operator might create on the server —
# `.env.production`, `.env.staging`, `.env.backup` — and then re-admits the one
# file that is meant to be committed.
#
# Order matters: the negation has to come after the pattern that would
# otherwise exclude it, or git never reconsiders the file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor, OS and log noise
#
# None of this exists in the tree today. It is here so that the first person to
# open the project in a different editor, or on a different operating system,
# does not commit their own machine's clutter into a repository that has been
# clean from its first commit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.log
